WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:02.300
So, um, picture this scenario for a second.

00:02.560 --> 00:02.820
Okay.

00:03.180 --> 00:05.380
You are sitting at your own kitchen table, right?

00:05.860 --> 00:07.220
It's six o'clock on a Tuesday.

00:07.780 --> 00:08.460
The witching hour.

00:08.960 --> 00:09.360
Exactly.

00:09.960 --> 00:13.940
You've just spent the last, I don't know, 45 minutes chopping, sautéing,

00:14.280 --> 00:20.260
carefully plating up what you consider to be a perfectly reasonable, objectively delicious meal.

00:20.420 --> 00:22.100
Right, like maybe a mild chicken stir-fry.

00:22.680 --> 00:25.240
Yeah, stir-fry with some bright green snow peas.

00:25.860 --> 00:28.400
You place the plate down in front of a three-year-old child.

00:28.400 --> 00:30.200
I can see it happening already.

00:30.560 --> 00:39.080
And you just watch as their expression shifts from, like, neutral to suspicious to outright visceral horror.

00:39.420 --> 00:40.420
Just absolute panic.

00:40.820 --> 00:41.260
Yes.

00:41.560 --> 00:47.900
They look at the snow pea the way you or I might look at a live grenade that someone just casually tossed onto our placemat.

00:48.100 --> 00:49.200
That is painfully accurate.

00:49.620 --> 00:51.400
And right there, the battle lines are drawn.

00:51.820 --> 00:53.740
The dining room descends into conflict.

00:53.740 --> 01:01.580
And if you ask anyone, like your parents, the pediatrician, the internet, what the problem is, you'll get a single, seemingly straightforward label.

01:02.080 --> 01:04.080
They'll just tell you that you have a picky eater.

01:04.480 --> 01:04.840
Exactly.

01:05.240 --> 01:05.980
A picky eater.

01:05.980 --> 01:08.860
It's a label that gets thrown around with absolute certainty.

01:09.080 --> 01:10.300
It feels like a diagnosis, even.

01:10.460 --> 01:10.800
Right.

01:10.880 --> 01:15.820
But usually when we talk about a diagnosis, there's a level of mechanical precision to it.

01:15.900 --> 01:16.400
Oh, for sure.

01:16.500 --> 01:22.200
Like, you break your arm, the x-ray shows a jagged white line on the bone, and the doctor points at the screen and says, there is the problem.

01:22.440 --> 01:23.040
It's clean.

01:23.260 --> 01:23.780
It's visible.

01:24.140 --> 01:24.960
You can point right at it.

01:25.100 --> 01:25.260
Yeah.

01:25.260 --> 01:31.880
But step into the behavioral landscape of a toddler refusing a vegetable, and that x-ray machine is utterly useless.

01:32.440 --> 01:32.860
Completely useless.

01:33.060 --> 01:38.280
The problem is incredibly murky, yet society treats it like it's this binary character flaw.

01:38.820 --> 01:41.820
And that is exactly what we are going to dismantle today.

01:42.340 --> 01:43.420
Welcome to this deep dive.

01:43.760 --> 01:44.800
I am so ready for this.

01:45.520 --> 01:52.680
Today's mission is really profound, because we're going to look at the very architecture of taste, curiosity, and human behavior.

01:52.680 --> 01:57.120
And we are doing that through the lens of a truly remarkable piece of source material.

01:57.540 --> 02:02.040
We're looking at a paper published in October 2026 by the independent researcher Flyxion.

02:02.560 --> 02:02.860
Yes.

02:03.020 --> 02:09.920
The title is Transformational Familiarity, Food Neophobia, Autonomy, and the Construction of Taste.

02:10.220 --> 02:12.840
It is an incredibly dense, multi-layered document.

02:13.040 --> 02:13.720
It really is.

02:14.080 --> 02:20.760
And what I love about it is that it doesn't just offer you a few, like, quick dinner table tips or tricks.

02:20.840 --> 02:21.020
No.

02:21.080 --> 02:22.080
It goes way deeper than that.

02:22.080 --> 02:25.440
It introduces a completely new theoretical framework.

02:25.640 --> 02:28.720
It's called the Transformational Familiarity Hypothesis.

02:28.820 --> 02:30.380
Which is such a fascinating concept.

02:30.520 --> 02:30.760
Right.

02:31.320 --> 02:36.020
This theory argues that food acceptance isn't a stubborn, fixed trait inside the eater's brain.

02:36.120 --> 02:40.940
Instead, it is a highly flexible, malleable feature of the environment itself.

02:41.580 --> 02:44.220
If we change the environment, we change the acceptance.

02:44.500 --> 02:45.440
Okay, let's unpack this.

02:45.520 --> 02:50.020
I mean, where do we even begin to tear down the old model of how we feed ourselves and our kids?

02:50.020 --> 02:53.180
Well, you really have to start by clearing away the bad terminology.

02:53.760 --> 02:57.960
Before we can solve a problem, we must define it with absolute precision.

02:58.160 --> 02:58.620
Makes sense.

02:58.620 --> 03:04.440
So, Fleisen's opening maneuver is to systematically dismantle that umbrella term, picky eater.

03:04.580 --> 03:05.100
Let's do it.

03:05.100 --> 03:11.880
The paper argues, and frankly quite convincingly, that this label isn't just unhelpful, it is actively harmful.

03:12.080 --> 03:12.900
Actively harmful.

03:13.040 --> 03:13.460
How so?

03:13.780 --> 03:22.420
Because it takes a wildly heterogeneous, diverse set of psychological and biological behaviors and just lumps them together as a single defect.

03:22.420 --> 03:23.300
I see.

03:23.460 --> 03:25.520
It's a catch-all that catches way too much.

03:25.640 --> 03:25.980
Exactly.

03:26.400 --> 03:33.300
The paper breaks down how the label picky actually obscures five entirely distinct, scientifically verified processes.

03:33.740 --> 03:34.820
Okay, what's the first one?

03:35.080 --> 03:36.480
Let's look at food neophobia.

03:37.320 --> 03:41.000
This is an evolutionary, deeply adaptive caution toward novelty.

03:41.560 --> 03:44.300
We have to think about this from a biological perspective.

03:44.480 --> 03:45.480
Okay, biology.

03:45.480 --> 03:53.680
For an omnivore, like a human being, exploring new foods is necessary to get a full spectrum of nutrition, but it's also incredibly dangerous.

03:54.160 --> 03:57.620
Right, because in the wild, new equals potentially poisonous.

03:58.300 --> 03:58.780
Precisely.

03:59.580 --> 04:08.520
If you look at the foundational research in this area, like work done by Paul Rosen decades ago, you see that the fear of new foods isn't just stubbornness.

04:08.680 --> 04:09.380
It's an instinct.

04:09.780 --> 04:11.200
It's a biological firewall.

04:11.200 --> 04:15.980
And what's fascinating is the timeline of when this fear usually peaks in human development.

04:16.440 --> 04:16.760
When is it?

04:17.320 --> 04:19.500
It spikes right when children become mobile.

04:19.940 --> 04:20.640
Oh, wow.

04:21.100 --> 04:24.660
Which makes perfect, terrifying sense when you really think about it.

04:24.820 --> 04:25.380
It really does.

04:25.460 --> 04:34.800
Like if you have a cave toddler who has just figured out how to walk, they are suddenly capable of wandering away from the safety of the fire and the watchful eye of the caregivers.

04:35.420 --> 04:35.940
Exactly.

04:35.940 --> 04:43.540
If they didn't have an innate fear of the unfamiliar, they would just be popping random, potentially lethal berries and mushrooms into their mouths.

04:43.900 --> 04:45.600
Neophobia is an ancient survival mechanism.

04:45.960 --> 04:47.440
It kept our ancestors alive.

04:48.320 --> 04:57.060
So when a modern child looks at an unfamiliar vegetable and refuses it, their brain is literally performing a safety check that has been hardwired by evolution.

04:57.380 --> 04:58.800
That reframes so much.

04:58.800 --> 05:01.960
Okay, but then we move to the second process, right?

05:02.060 --> 05:04.000
Which is completely different from neophobia?

05:04.780 --> 05:04.980
Yes.

05:05.260 --> 05:07.580
The second one is sensory aversion.

05:08.040 --> 05:09.380
Wait, break down the difference for me.

05:09.460 --> 05:13.740
Because to the frustrated adult holding the spoon, they both just look like a kid saying no.

05:14.180 --> 05:14.880
That's the trap.

05:15.420 --> 05:17.960
The difference lies in the mechanism of the projection.

05:18.760 --> 05:21.000
Neophobia is about the novelty of the item.

05:21.580 --> 05:24.280
I won't eat it because I do not know what it is.

05:24.740 --> 05:25.060
Okay.

05:25.060 --> 05:32.100
Sensory aversion, on the other hand, is a negative physiological response to a specific property of the food.

05:32.460 --> 05:32.940
Like what?

05:33.380 --> 05:37.820
Its texture, its temperature, a specific bitter flavor profile.

05:38.620 --> 05:44.460
And crucially, unlike neophobia, a sensory aversion does not just go away once the food becomes familiar.

05:45.100 --> 05:45.720
Oh, I see.

05:45.880 --> 05:54.220
A child can be exposed to a food a hundred times, know exactly what it is, and still experience intense sensory distress when it enters their mouth.

05:54.220 --> 05:55.800
That sounds miserable.

05:56.240 --> 05:56.560
It is.

05:57.080 --> 06:03.900
We see this commonly documented in populations with autism spectrum disorders, where sensory processing can be highly acute.

06:04.360 --> 06:07.140
So neophobia is, I won't eat it because it's new.

06:07.520 --> 06:11.620
And sensory aversion is, I won't eat it because it feels like wet sand in my mouth.

06:11.720 --> 06:15.680
And no matter how many times you show it to me, it will always feel like wet sand.

06:16.040 --> 06:16.980
That is exactly it.

06:17.200 --> 06:17.400
Wow.

06:17.580 --> 06:17.780
Okay.

06:18.000 --> 06:21.040
And if we move down Flyxion's list, what's the third process?

06:21.040 --> 06:24.900
The third is what's called a stable preference for familiar foods.

06:25.540 --> 06:25.780
Meaning?

06:26.460 --> 06:28.580
It means this isn't pathological at all.

06:29.060 --> 06:32.540
Some children, and plenty of adults for that matter, are just creatures of habit.

06:32.680 --> 06:33.580
I can relate to that.

06:33.920 --> 06:34.140
Right.

06:34.300 --> 06:36.660
They might be perfectly willing to taste something new.

06:37.140 --> 06:38.660
They don't have an intense fear of it.

06:38.980 --> 06:42.420
But they just consistently prefer the comfort of their established repertoire.

06:43.020 --> 06:44.560
So they just like what they like.

06:44.800 --> 06:45.200
Exactly.

06:45.200 --> 06:50.640
This is a behavioral baseline that naturally tends to broaden as a person ages and their palate matures.

06:51.280 --> 06:52.780
Just a standard preference for the known.

06:53.040 --> 06:53.900
Nothing broken there.

06:54.020 --> 06:54.900
What's the fourth one?

06:55.320 --> 06:57.460
The fourth is ordinary variation in appetite.

06:58.100 --> 07:00.620
And honestly, this is a massive source of misunderstanding.

07:01.160 --> 07:01.520
How so?

07:01.980 --> 07:04.200
Well, adults generally eat three meals a day.

07:04.500 --> 07:08.040
And we regulate our caloric intake on a roughly 24-hour cycle.

07:08.860 --> 07:10.880
Young children do not operate that way.

07:11.140 --> 07:11.700
They don't?

07:11.940 --> 07:12.400
No.

07:12.400 --> 07:17.400
Their bodies regulate energy intake across several days, not single meals.

07:17.900 --> 07:20.660
They are constantly cycling through growth spurts and plateaus.

07:21.040 --> 07:22.440
Oh, that makes so much sense.

07:22.820 --> 07:27.260
So a child who completely refuses dinner on a Thursday might not be picky at all.

07:27.560 --> 07:28.000
Not at all.

07:28.220 --> 07:33.580
They might just be between growth spurts, or maybe they had a huge calorie-dense snack at three in the afternoon,

07:33.720 --> 07:36.660
and their body is simply saying, we are at capacity.

07:36.840 --> 07:38.200
That's exactly what's happening.

07:38.200 --> 07:45.520
But the adult sitting at the table panics, sees the untouched plate, and immediately slaps the picky eater label on them.

07:45.940 --> 07:52.160
We misinterpret a biological reality, which is just a lack of hunger, as a behavioral defiance.

07:52.440 --> 07:54.280
Which is so unfair to the kid.

07:54.480 --> 07:54.920
It is.

07:55.360 --> 08:00.580
Which leads us directly into the fifth process on Flaxer's list, resistance to coercion.

08:00.700 --> 08:02.460
Ooh, resistance to coercion.

08:02.460 --> 08:02.900
Yes.

08:03.340 --> 08:07.420
This is pure psychological reactance against pressure.

08:07.980 --> 08:14.160
When a child refuses a food for any of the first four reasons, adults often introduce intense mealtime dynamics.

08:14.380 --> 08:14.620
Right.

08:14.720 --> 08:15.540
The bargaining phase.

08:15.880 --> 08:16.440
We bribe.

08:16.720 --> 08:17.300
We negotiate.

08:17.540 --> 08:17.880
We threaten.

08:18.000 --> 08:18.500
We hover.

08:19.460 --> 08:21.580
This creates a terrible feedback loop.

08:22.160 --> 08:23.240
How does the loop work?

08:23.240 --> 08:29.780
The child's refusal becomes a defense of their own bodily autonomy, rather than a reflection of the food itself.

08:30.320 --> 08:34.160
The adult's remedy actually sustains and deepens the problem.

08:34.980 --> 08:36.020
That is wild.

08:36.500 --> 08:46.140
And just to be completely thorough for you listening, Flaxham does carve out a sixth, entirely distinct category that sits outside of this standard behavioral framework, right?

08:46.200 --> 08:48.140
Yes, that distinction is absolutely vital.

08:48.140 --> 08:55.720
These are clinically significant feeding disorders, like avoidant restrictive food intake disorder, commonly known as ARFID.

08:55.960 --> 08:56.340
Exactly.

08:57.020 --> 09:04.840
ARFID involves significant, dangerous weight loss, severe nutritional deficiency, or a total disruption of psychosocial functioning.

09:05.160 --> 09:06.680
So it's not just being fussy.

09:06.960 --> 09:07.680
No, not at all.

09:07.880 --> 09:11.100
It requires highly specialized medical and psychiatric intervention.

09:11.100 --> 09:20.080
The theoretical framework we are discussing today is aimed at the vast, non-clinical majority of feeding challenges, not acute medical disorders.

09:20.600 --> 09:20.960
Got it.

09:21.280 --> 09:32.600
But looking at those five main categories, neophobia, sensory aversion, stable preference, appetite variation, and resistance to coercion, it really hits you like a ton of bricks.

09:32.800 --> 09:34.280
It completely changes the landscape.

09:34.280 --> 09:41.860
We are taking five distinct psychological and biological phenomena and trying to treat them all with the exact same playbook.

09:42.380 --> 09:42.640
Yes.

09:43.160 --> 09:44.480
I keep thinking about a car mechanic.

09:44.840 --> 09:49.200
If you take your car to the shop and you just say, it's broken, the mechanic can't do anything with that.

09:49.560 --> 09:52.180
The diagnosis has to dictate the intervention.

09:52.600 --> 09:52.780
Right.

09:53.040 --> 09:53.980
The car could be out of gas.

09:54.440 --> 09:56.420
That's the ordinary variation in appetite.

09:56.640 --> 09:59.720
You just need to wait until the tank is empty and it will run again.

09:59.900 --> 10:00.580
Perfect analogy.

10:01.380 --> 10:03.600
Or the car could be missing a wheel.

10:04.280 --> 10:05.700
That is a sensory aversion.

10:06.060 --> 10:08.800
The mechanics of the mouth literally cannot process the texture.

10:09.180 --> 10:09.500
Exactly.

10:10.020 --> 10:12.760
Or you might be slamming your foot on the brake instead of the gas pedal.

10:12.980 --> 10:14.440
That's the resistance to coercion.

10:14.680 --> 10:14.940
Yes.

10:15.360 --> 10:24.380
If you have a car that is missing a wheel and the mechanic just keeps trying to pour more gas into it, not only are you not going anywhere, but you are going to ruin the car.

10:24.740 --> 10:30.340
That is a phenomenal analogy for what is happening at millions of dinner tables every single night.

10:30.700 --> 10:31.760
It's just endless frustration.

10:31.760 --> 10:37.200
If you misdiagnose a sensory aversion as neophobia, your intervention will be disastrous.

10:37.920 --> 10:43.420
Because what's fascinating here is the standard remedy for neophobia is repeated low pressure exposure.

10:43.760 --> 10:45.300
Just keep putting it on the plate.

10:45.580 --> 10:45.800
Right.

10:45.800 --> 10:51.660
But if you do that to a child with a severe sensory aversion, you are not curing a fear of the unknown.

10:52.160 --> 10:55.060
Because they know what it is and it physically bothers them.

10:55.280 --> 10:55.580
Exactly.

10:56.420 --> 11:03.380
You are repeatedly forcing a child to engage with a texture that their brain is actively registering as intolerable.

11:03.380 --> 11:09.240
You are causing active, repeated distress under the guise of helping them learn.

11:09.820 --> 11:10.200
Ouch.

11:10.440 --> 11:17.140
But I want to pause and push back on something regarding neophobia, specifically the biological genetic side of it.

11:17.220 --> 11:17.380
Sure.

11:17.520 --> 11:17.900
Let's hear it.

11:18.180 --> 11:23.780
Because if we look at the paper, Phylaxian cites this really fascinating 2007 twin study by Cook and colleagues.

11:23.780 --> 11:24.740
Yes, the twin study.

11:25.140 --> 11:30.120
And the study estimates that the heritability of food neophobia is 0.78.

11:30.220 --> 11:31.800
That is 78%.

11:31.800 --> 11:32.880
That's a huge number.

11:33.360 --> 11:42.260
If nearly 80% of a child's fear of new food is hardwired into their genetics, aren't parents just fighting an unwinnable genetic battle?

11:42.260 --> 11:49.060
I mean, are some people just genetically destined to eat plain pasta and chicken nuggets until they are adults no matter what we do?

11:49.660 --> 11:54.840
It is a critical question and it addresses one of the most common misunderstandings of statistical science.

11:55.200 --> 11:56.360
Okay, correct me.

11:56.980 --> 12:05.040
Phylaxian is very careful to clarify exactly what that 0.78 figure actually means in the context of behavioral genetics.

12:05.040 --> 12:16.720
Heritability does not mean that 78% of a single individual child's behavior is locked in by their DNA, setting some unmovable hard ceiling on what they can achieve.

12:17.200 --> 12:21.160
Walk me through that, because to a layperson, 78% sounds like a life sentence.

12:21.320 --> 12:22.020
Give me a comparison.

12:22.840 --> 12:23.780
Think about human height.

12:24.100 --> 12:24.360
Okay.

12:24.960 --> 12:27.840
Height is highly heritable, even more so than neophobia.

12:27.840 --> 12:34.560
If you look at a population, genetic differences explain a huge percentage of why some people are taller than others.

12:34.560 --> 12:36.420
Right. Tall parents usually have tall kids.

12:36.700 --> 12:37.020
Exactly.

12:37.420 --> 12:45.820
However, if you take a population and radically change their environment, say by vastly improving childhood nutrition and eliminating early childhood diseases,

12:46.140 --> 12:50.760
the average height of that entire population will shoot up across a few generations.

12:51.040 --> 12:51.560
Oh, I see.

12:52.040 --> 12:55.200
The genetics didn't change in that short time, but the environment did.

12:55.980 --> 13:00.320
Heritability explains the share of variation within a given population at a given time.

13:00.320 --> 13:00.940
Oh, okay.

13:01.500 --> 13:05.920
So it's a population-level metric for variance, not an individual destiny score.

13:06.140 --> 13:06.600
Precisely.

13:07.260 --> 13:15.740
In the context of neophobia, it means that genetic differences explain a large part of why children differ from one another in their baseline reluctance to try new things.

13:15.980 --> 13:22.480
So one child might start at a baseline of extreme caution while another child is naturally more adventurous.

13:22.480 --> 13:34.000
Exactly. But the environment, the food culture of the home, the way meals are structured, still has a massive, tractable role to play in shifting both of those children toward greater acceptance.

13:34.480 --> 13:37.820
The environment can pull the levers that the genetics built.

13:38.280 --> 13:39.480
Which is the perfect pivot.

13:40.100 --> 13:47.820
If we accept that picky eating is actually five different things and that we aren't completely helpless prisoners to genetics, we have to look at the environment.

13:47.980 --> 13:48.280
We do.

13:48.280 --> 13:57.440
Specifically, we need to look at what science has historically prescribed to fix this and why, frankly, it sometimes fails so spectacularly.

13:57.760 --> 14:03.840
And Fleischer dedicates a significant portion of the paper to meticulously reviewing this established literature.

14:04.120 --> 14:06.080
Because there's a lot of advice out there.

14:06.220 --> 14:06.840
Endless advice.

14:07.500 --> 14:13.960
But for decades, pediatricians, psychologists, and dieticians have basically been handing out the same three pillars of advice.

14:13.960 --> 14:16.180
Expose them to the food repeatedly.

14:16.880 --> 14:18.560
Model eating the food yourself.

14:19.260 --> 14:21.160
And whatever you do, do not pressure them.

14:21.480 --> 14:23.300
The holy trinity of mealtime advice.

14:23.660 --> 14:24.140
Exactly.

14:24.560 --> 14:26.060
Let's break down the FOST pillar.

14:26.660 --> 14:27.140
Exposure.

14:27.580 --> 14:30.060
Because this seems to be the most common piece of advice you hear.

14:30.280 --> 14:31.980
Just keep putting it on their plate.

14:32.120 --> 14:34.000
Eventually, they will break down and eat it.

14:34.240 --> 14:37.920
The scientific foundation for this is actually incredibly robust.

14:37.920 --> 14:45.940
It is based on the mere exposure effect, which was originally described by the psychologist Robert Zajonk in 1968.

14:46.720 --> 14:47.700
What did Zajonk find?

14:48.180 --> 14:58.400
He found that simply exposing a person repeatedly to a novel stimulus, whether it was a geometric shape, a foreign word, or a melody, increased their baseline preference for it.

14:58.600 --> 15:00.100
Just through sheer repetition.

15:00.620 --> 15:00.880
Yes.

15:00.880 --> 15:05.760
The brain learns that the stimulus is not a threat, and familiarity breeds comfort.

15:06.540 --> 15:13.320
And then in 1982, researchers Birch and Marlin successfully applied this exact concept to food acceptance in children.

15:13.780 --> 15:16.000
So, just getting the reps in.

15:16.600 --> 15:20.820
You taste it ten times, your brain realizes you haven't died, and it stops sounding the alarm.

15:21.360 --> 15:21.600
Yes.

15:21.820 --> 15:24.000
The mechanism of tasting absolutely works.

15:24.820 --> 15:29.460
But here is the crucial nuance that often gets lost in translation.

15:29.460 --> 15:33.120
What kind of exposure actually moves the needle?

15:33.480 --> 15:33.500
Okay.

15:33.780 --> 15:33.980
Yeah.

15:34.640 --> 15:37.320
Is it enough to just have the food sit there on the plate?

15:38.420 --> 15:44.840
Birch did another pivotal study in 1987 where they separated looking at novel fruits from actually tasting them.

15:44.960 --> 15:46.140
What kind of novel fruits?

15:46.560 --> 15:50.980
They used things that North American children in the 80s would likely have never seen.

15:51.320 --> 15:55.520
Things like lychee, rambutan, or starfruit.

15:55.760 --> 15:56.300
Oh, okay.

15:56.300 --> 16:04.360
The researchers found that simply looking at the novel food repeatedly did increase the children's visual preference for it.

16:04.940 --> 16:07.080
They liked how it looked more.

16:08.080 --> 16:11.060
But it did absolutely nothing to increase their taste preference.

16:11.840 --> 16:16.340
Only the children who repeatedly tasted the food developed a preference for eating it.

16:16.760 --> 16:17.780
That is fascinating.

16:17.780 --> 16:25.200
So the advice to just leave the broccoli sitting untouched on the edge of the plate so they get used to seeing it is fundamentally flawed?

16:25.700 --> 16:25.900
Yes.

16:26.140 --> 16:29.800
They are just getting used to it as a table decoration, not as sustenance?

16:30.520 --> 16:31.400
Historically, yes.

16:31.740 --> 16:33.760
Visual exposure alone is very weak.

16:34.380 --> 16:41.400
Flyxion does point out that more recent interventions in nursery schools have shown that multi-sensory play can bridge this gap.

16:41.600 --> 16:43.080
Oh, like playing with their food?

16:43.080 --> 16:44.840
Basically, yes.

16:45.740 --> 16:58.220
If you have children physically interact with the food, feeling the texture of a kiwi, smelling herbs, painting with beet juice, that tactile and olfactory engagement does increase the likelihood of later touching and tasting.

16:58.740 --> 17:02.400
But passive looking is insufficient to overcome neophobia.

17:03.360 --> 17:05.080
Okay, so exposure has limits.

17:05.080 --> 17:07.700
What about the second pillar, social modeling?

17:08.180 --> 17:13.400
This is the classic, look at mommy, mommy loves these peas so much routine.

17:14.100 --> 17:17.060
Every parent has done this one-person Broadway show at the dinner table.

17:17.220 --> 17:17.940
Guilty as charged.

17:18.280 --> 17:21.280
And it is rooted in very solid developmental psychology.

17:21.640 --> 17:24.560
Albert Bandura's social learning theory from the 1970s.

17:24.580 --> 17:24.800
Right.

17:25.020 --> 17:26.060
Monkey see, monkey do.

17:26.220 --> 17:26.660
Exactly.

17:27.160 --> 17:28.720
Children are observational learners.

17:28.720 --> 17:35.260
Watching a trusted adult eat a food provides vital information to the child's brain that the food is safe to ingest.

17:36.040 --> 17:38.960
But the manner in which that modeling happens is everything.

17:39.300 --> 17:39.720
How so?

17:40.060 --> 17:44.180
Flyxion highlights a fascinating study by Hendy and Raudenbush from the year 2000.

17:44.480 --> 17:45.080
What did they do?

17:45.480 --> 17:49.440
They observed preschool children and tested different styles of adult modeling.

17:49.440 --> 17:59.240
They found that silent modeling, an adult just sitting there eating the novel food without making a fuss about it, did absolutely nothing to encourage the children to accept it.

17:59.460 --> 18:00.240
It didn't work at all.

18:00.420 --> 18:00.580
Nope.

18:01.240 --> 18:11.100
However, enthusiastic modeling, with the adult actively communicated positive engagement and pleasure, maintained the children's acceptance of the new food across five consecutive meals.

18:11.320 --> 18:13.000
So you really do have to sell it.

18:13.320 --> 18:14.980
The Broadway performance actually works.

18:15.160 --> 18:15.980
You have to sell it.

18:15.980 --> 18:22.320
The child's brain is looking for affective cues, emotional signals about the safety and value of the object.

18:22.520 --> 18:23.960
But this brings us to the danger zone.

18:24.100 --> 18:24.460
Uh-oh.

18:24.740 --> 18:32.280
Because enthusiastic modeling frequently collides with the third pillar of traditional advice, the mandate to avoid pressure and restriction.

18:32.460 --> 18:33.440
Which is so hard to do.

18:33.700 --> 18:37.160
When you want them to eat it, the pressure just naturally builds.

18:37.440 --> 18:41.020
What does the literature actually say about pressuring kids to eat?

18:41.320 --> 18:44.700
The literature is incredibly clear and deeply cautionary.

18:44.700 --> 18:46.300
Let's look at restriction first.

18:47.100 --> 19:00.440
A famous 1999 study by Fisher and Birch demonstrated that restricting a child's access to appealing foods, for example, putting the cookies on a high shelf and making them a forbidden reward, does not make the child want them less.

19:01.480 --> 19:03.420
It makes those foods hypersalient.

19:03.920 --> 19:06.060
It artificially inflates their desirability.

19:06.440 --> 19:08.240
The forbidden fruit effect in action.

19:08.520 --> 19:11.120
If you tell me I can't have it, it is the only thing I can think about.

19:11.160 --> 19:11.520
Exactly.

19:11.520 --> 19:13.680
And the flip side is just as powerful.

19:14.240 --> 19:19.900
Pressuring a child to eat a healthy food actively creates lasting, durable aversions.

19:20.360 --> 19:20.700
Really?

19:20.920 --> 19:21.120
Mm-hmm.

19:21.280 --> 19:22.660
Like long-term?

19:23.020 --> 19:23.340
Yes.

19:23.820 --> 19:28.420
A retrospective study by Batcell in 2002 surveyed adults about their food aversions.

19:28.920 --> 19:30.520
They found a massive correlation.

19:31.240 --> 19:38.200
Adults frequently hold lifelong visceral hatreds for the specific foods they were pressured or forced to eat as children.

19:38.480 --> 19:39.080
Oh, wow.

19:39.080 --> 19:44.820
The pressure doesn't just ruin the meal. It can permanently rewire their effective response to that ingredient.

19:45.060 --> 19:49.600
Okay, let's look at how all these pillars interact because this feels like a trap.

19:49.720 --> 19:50.580
It really is a trap.

19:50.860 --> 20:08.380
If I am enthusiastically eating broccoli, doing the whole, mmm, this is amazing routine, but I am also relentlessly putting it on a child's plate every single night, and that child has a severe sensory aversion to the texture of broccoli, I am sending wildly mixed signals.

20:08.380 --> 20:09.060
You are.

20:09.300 --> 20:15.480
I'm trying to model enthusiastically, but my relentless exposure is essentially functioning as pressure.

20:15.580 --> 20:16.840
I am backing them into a corner.

20:17.040 --> 20:17.380
Exactly.

20:17.740 --> 20:22.900
How does a parent or any caregiver know when to stop exposing and start adapting?

20:23.300 --> 20:29.140
You have just articulated the exact blind spot that Fleece identifies in the traditional scientific literature.

20:29.140 --> 20:36.600
If you look closely at standard exposure studies, they're almost entirely focused on changing the child's response to a fixed food.

20:36.960 --> 20:37.040
Right.

20:37.420 --> 20:37.660
Right.

20:37.980 --> 20:39.980
The broccoli is the unchangeable constant.

20:40.180 --> 20:40.540
Exactly.

20:40.960 --> 20:47.160
You hold the target food constant, and you try to hammer the child's resistance down through sheer repetition and modeling.

20:47.520 --> 20:50.080
You expect the child to do 100% of the adapting.

20:50.080 --> 20:52.960
Which we just learned can cause permanent aversions.

20:53.140 --> 20:53.400
Right.

20:54.120 --> 20:55.980
So Fleece, he asks a revolutionary question.

20:56.740 --> 20:59.540
What if we stop trying to force the child to adapt to the food?

21:00.200 --> 21:03.600
What if instead we change the pathway to the food itself?

21:03.940 --> 21:10.660
And this is where the paper just takes off into the stratosphere, because to change the pathway to a food, we can't just look at nutrition.

21:10.660 --> 21:13.880
We have to dive deep into cognitive psychology and philosophy.

21:13.880 --> 21:15.500
Yes, we do.

21:15.660 --> 21:24.360
Before we can figure out how to successfully introduce a new food, we have to understand how the human brain actually categorizes what is and isn't food in the first place.

21:24.660 --> 21:27.620
This is one of the most intellectually thrilling sections of the paper.

21:28.280 --> 21:32.520
Flaxseen bridges nutritional science with deep philosophical concepts.

21:32.840 --> 21:34.140
I couldn't believe it when I read it.

21:34.140 --> 21:46.220
To explain how a child recognizes a food, the paper brings in the work of Eleanor Roche, a pioneer in psychological categorization, and Ludwig Wittgenstein, the towering early 20th century philosopher.

21:46.700 --> 21:51.400
I have to say, when I saw Wittgenstein referenced in a paper about toddler feeding dynamics, I laughed out loud.

21:51.720 --> 21:53.440
It is a bit unexpected, isn't it?

21:53.460 --> 21:56.420
We are bringing high-level philosophy of language to the high chair.

21:56.580 --> 21:57.220
But it works.

21:57.420 --> 22:00.260
Walk me through how Wittgenstein applies to a plate of spaghetti.

22:00.740 --> 22:02.380
It is incredibly relevant.

22:02.380 --> 22:09.080
But Wittgenstein's major contribution here is his observation about how humans group things together.

22:10.320 --> 22:15.820
He noted that we don't categorize the world based on strict, rigid dictionary definitions.

22:16.320 --> 22:19.660
His famous example is the concept of a game.

22:20.280 --> 22:20.380
Right.

22:21.160 --> 22:22.000
Think about it.

22:22.380 --> 22:26.100
What is the single universal feature that defines all games?

22:26.220 --> 22:26.560
Exactly.

22:26.820 --> 22:27.300
What is it?

22:27.440 --> 22:32.360
A board game like chess, a card game like poker, a physical game like soccer, a solitary game like...

22:32.360 --> 22:33.020
Solitaire.

22:34.260 --> 22:36.820
There is no single common denominator.

22:37.140 --> 22:37.820
There really isn't.

22:38.000 --> 22:39.120
Some have winners and losers.

22:39.300 --> 22:39.780
Some don't.

22:39.880 --> 22:40.500
Some use equipment.

22:40.620 --> 22:40.980
Some don't.

22:41.060 --> 22:41.600
Some are physical.

22:41.780 --> 22:42.700
Some are purely mental.

22:42.920 --> 22:43.260
Exactly.

22:44.200 --> 22:46.120
So how do we know they are all games?

22:47.340 --> 22:51.660
Wittgenstein argued that categories are held together by a network of overlapping similarities.

22:52.460 --> 22:54.680
He called this family resemblance.

22:54.680 --> 22:56.040
Family resemblance.

22:56.400 --> 22:56.520
Yep.

22:56.720 --> 23:01.760
Two people are from the same family because they share a nose or a certain laugh or a gait.

23:02.440 --> 23:05.680
Even if there is no single feature that all 50 family members share.

23:06.140 --> 23:06.420
Right.

23:06.500 --> 23:08.100
They just sort of look related.

23:08.800 --> 23:13.380
Applied to cognitive psychology by Eleanor Roche, this means we think in terms of prototypes.

23:13.820 --> 23:17.420
We hold a central, idealized image of a category.

23:17.420 --> 23:21.260
And we judge new items based on how closely they resemble that prototype.

23:21.260 --> 23:23.440
Okay, so let's apply this to the dining room.

23:24.040 --> 23:28.180
A child does not have a rigid scientific definition of what a pizza is.

23:28.540 --> 23:34.340
They don't have a mental checklist that says must contain gluten, must contain dairy, must be baked at 400 degrees.

23:34.480 --> 23:34.640
No.

23:35.400 --> 23:36.480
They hold a prototype.

23:37.360 --> 23:40.500
For most North American children, the prototypical pizza is round.

23:41.020 --> 23:42.560
It has a flat baked crust.

23:42.920 --> 23:44.920
It features a red tomato sauce.

23:45.040 --> 23:47.140
And it is topped with melted stretchy cheese.

23:47.140 --> 23:54.180
And an item is a better or worse member of the pizza category based on how many of those features it shares.

23:54.800 --> 23:56.980
The boundaries of the category are soft and fuzzy.

23:57.560 --> 23:59.460
A square slice of pizza is still pizza.

23:59.800 --> 24:01.980
It's just a slightly less prototypical version.

24:02.320 --> 24:02.660
Exactly.

24:03.440 --> 24:08.180
But here is why this philosophical detour is absolutely crucial for feeding kids.

24:08.920 --> 24:12.240
What happens when those fuzzy boundaries are violently violated?

24:13.020 --> 24:13.880
The kid freaks out.

24:13.880 --> 24:19.540
This isn't just a matter of a child saying, hmm, I don't prefer this variation on the pizza prototype.

24:20.060 --> 24:22.020
The reaction is much more visceral.

24:22.700 --> 24:25.640
Right, because of what the brain associates with category violations.

24:26.400 --> 24:31.160
This is where Flyxion draws heavily on Paul Rosen's seminal work on the semantics of disgust.

24:32.200 --> 24:38.520
For human beings, the boundary between what is food and what is non-food is not emotionally neutral.

24:38.940 --> 24:39.780
It's life or death.

24:40.000 --> 24:42.400
It is heavily, effectively loaded.

24:42.400 --> 24:44.180
It is a matter of life and death.

24:45.000 --> 24:52.900
If a food violates a core category expectation, let's say you serve a child a familiar dish, but it has a completely unexpected color.

24:53.260 --> 24:54.620
Say, a blue french fry.

24:55.040 --> 24:58.100
The child doesn't just reject it as disliked.

24:58.360 --> 25:02.100
They don't think, oh, I prefer yellow fries, but I will eat this blue one.

25:02.320 --> 25:02.520
No.

25:03.060 --> 25:05.180
They reject it as contaminated.

25:05.180 --> 25:08.480
They reject it as literally disgusting.

25:09.140 --> 25:12.920
The unexpected color triggers an ancient alarm system.

25:13.760 --> 25:15.420
Because blue food is usually rotten food.

25:15.680 --> 25:16.080
Precisely.

25:16.580 --> 25:20.540
To the child's brain, a blue french fry isn't a weird french fry.

25:20.880 --> 25:21.620
It is poison.

25:21.960 --> 25:22.740
It is rot.

25:23.380 --> 25:27.080
They interpret the category violation as a profound signal of danger.

25:27.080 --> 25:31.360
And this explains so much about why kids flip out over the tiniest changes.

25:31.800 --> 25:35.120
The brand of chicken nuggets changes, and the breading is slightly darker.

25:35.700 --> 25:36.620
To us, it's a nugget.

25:36.860 --> 25:41.020
To them, the category has been breached, and it is no longer safe to eat.

25:41.360 --> 25:41.780
Exactly.

25:41.780 --> 25:46.460
Now, clinically, feeding therapists have intuitively known this for a while, right?

25:46.780 --> 25:48.740
The paper mentions food chaining.

25:49.320 --> 25:49.540
Yes.

25:49.980 --> 25:55.400
Phylaxion cites a 2007 clinical technique called food chaining, developed by Fraker and colleagues.

25:55.940 --> 26:01.940
This technique uses sensory similarities to link a child's accepted foods to new target foods.

26:02.440 --> 26:04.060
How does that look in practice?

26:04.640 --> 26:08.340
If a child only eats a specific brand of fast food chicken nuggets,

26:08.340 --> 26:12.420
a therapist might slowly transition them to a different brand of nuggets,

26:12.680 --> 26:16.280
then to a breaded fish stick, then to an unbreaded piece of chicken.

26:16.760 --> 26:18.460
You follow the family resemblance.

26:18.900 --> 26:19.460
Exactly.

26:19.640 --> 26:21.680
You exploit the overlapping features.

26:22.580 --> 26:24.920
However, as Phylaxion rightly points out,

26:25.260 --> 26:28.120
while therapists have used this successfully in clinical settings,

26:28.600 --> 26:30.760
it has largely been an art form.

26:31.180 --> 26:33.180
An art form, meaning it's subjective.

26:33.660 --> 26:37.180
It has lacked a formal mathematical theoretical structure.

26:37.180 --> 26:39.840
It relied entirely on the intuition of the therapist.

26:40.660 --> 26:43.620
And this is the moment in the deep dive where everything shifts.

26:44.100 --> 26:47.600
Because Phylaxion takes this philosophical idea of family resemblance,

26:48.060 --> 26:50.300
this clinical art of food chaining,

26:50.520 --> 26:52.660
and turns it into hard mathematics.

26:53.220 --> 26:54.240
It's quite a leap.

26:54.600 --> 26:55.460
I love this section.

26:55.900 --> 27:00.840
To help visualize it, I like to think of a specific food like a track on a massive audio mixing board.

27:01.020 --> 27:04.020
You know those big consoles in recording studios with all the vertical sliders?

27:04.340 --> 27:05.260
A perfect analogy.

27:05.460 --> 27:06.140
Let's map it out.

27:06.140 --> 27:10.000
So, every food has sliders for different dimensions.

27:10.540 --> 27:14.840
You have a slider for flavor profile, a slider for texture, a slider for aroma,

27:15.140 --> 27:21.120
a slider for visual presentation, and a slider for context, meaning where and how you are eating it.

27:21.220 --> 27:21.920
Okay. I'm with you.

27:22.400 --> 27:27.540
If you have a child whose absolute favorite safe food is a plain dry cracker,

27:28.120 --> 27:30.000
their sliders are set very low.

27:30.340 --> 27:33.340
Low flavor, dry texture, neutral visual.

27:33.340 --> 27:33.860
Right.

27:34.100 --> 27:37.760
If you suddenly take away the cracker and serve them a steaming bowl of spicy chili,

27:38.080 --> 27:39.220
what have you done?

27:39.860 --> 27:45.680
You have basically slammed every single slider up to the maximum level at the exact same time.

27:45.860 --> 27:48.140
And what happens when you push all the sliders to max?

27:48.700 --> 27:49.920
You blow the speakers.

27:49.920 --> 27:53.020
The child's sensory system is overwhelmed.

27:53.020 --> 27:57.960
The category of safe food is completely violated, and they reject it entirely.

27:57.960 --> 28:03.580
That audio mixing board is a brilliant, intuitive way to understand Flakeson's formal vector equation.

28:04.320 --> 28:11.460
The paper literally writes this out mathematically as X equals F, T, A, V, C.

28:11.460 --> 28:13.260
Okay. Breaking that down.

28:14.020 --> 28:17.920
Every single food preparation in the world is a vector, an X.

28:18.680 --> 28:22.240
And that vector is composed of its specific coordinates on those five dimensions.

28:22.700 --> 28:28.320
F for flavor, T for texture, A for aroma, V for visual, and C for context.

28:28.580 --> 28:32.800
Okay. So a strawberry has its own unique five-coordinate vector.

28:33.200 --> 28:35.060
A piece of toast has its own vector.

28:35.480 --> 28:35.900
Exactly.

28:35.900 --> 28:39.100
But here is the true genius of the mathematical model.

28:39.720 --> 28:42.660
It doesn't just map the food, it maps the child.

28:43.020 --> 28:43.960
It maps the child.

28:44.320 --> 28:48.120
The child has specific individual weights for each of those five dimensions.

28:48.520 --> 28:51.380
In the math, these weights are represented by the letter W.

28:52.040 --> 28:56.840
So we have WF for flavor weight, WT for texture weight, and so on.

28:57.060 --> 28:59.700
What does a weight actually mean in reality?

29:00.280 --> 29:05.000
It represents the child's personal sensitivity and rigidity on that specific dimension.

29:05.000 --> 29:08.540
Let's say you have a highly texture-sensitive child.

29:09.320 --> 29:11.940
In the math, they have a very heavy WT.

29:12.600 --> 29:15.220
Their texture slider is incredibly sensitive.

29:15.560 --> 29:17.080
Meaning they notice any change.

29:17.340 --> 29:17.540
Right.

29:17.900 --> 29:22.700
If you bump that slider even a millimeter, if a yogurt is slightly runnier than yesterday,

29:22.860 --> 29:26.540
or if there is a single microscopic seed in their strawberry puree,

29:27.100 --> 29:29.520
they notice it instantly and they reject the whole bowl.

29:29.840 --> 29:32.580
But another child might have a low WT.

29:32.580 --> 29:38.040
They don't care about texture at all, but they have a massive WV, a huge visual weight.

29:38.320 --> 29:38.440
Right.

29:38.920 --> 29:44.140
If the sandwich is cut into rectangles instead of triangles, the visual vector has moved too far.

29:44.560 --> 29:48.180
The heavy weight pulls it out of the acceptable category and they panic.

29:48.440 --> 29:50.040
This is blowing my mind a little bit.

29:50.400 --> 29:50.580
Okay.

29:50.680 --> 29:56.860
So with this math in place, what is the transformational familiarity hypothesis, formerly defined?

29:56.860 --> 30:04.600
The hypothesis states that you can move a child from a currently accepted safe food to a previously rejected target food

30:04.600 --> 30:07.680
by creating what Flyxion calls a candidate chain.

30:08.200 --> 30:09.040
A candidate chain.

30:09.480 --> 30:12.820
This is a highly calculated sequence of food preparations.

30:13.560 --> 30:18.820
In each step of the chain, you only move one or two of those sliders a tiny bit at a time.

30:18.980 --> 30:20.900
You change the vectors incrementally.

30:21.120 --> 30:21.440
Exactly.

30:21.440 --> 30:26.820
Mathematically, each step in the sequence must be geometrically close to the preceding step.

30:27.540 --> 30:32.820
And crucially, every step must be nutritionally acceptable to the adult, safe for the child,

30:33.280 --> 30:37.340
and offered completely transparently as a variant of the last step.

30:37.720 --> 30:39.720
This is where theory hits the frying pan.

30:40.160 --> 30:44.440
Let's walk through the paper's primary worked example because it is masterful.

30:45.260 --> 30:46.020
It really is.

30:46.020 --> 30:53.940
The goal of this hypothetical chain is to get a highly selective child to eat a pizza that utilizes a soft, cooked pasta crust.

30:54.240 --> 30:54.360
Right.

30:55.000 --> 31:00.560
And to a child who only eats dry snacks, a pasta crust pizza looks like an alien life form.

31:01.240 --> 31:03.740
It violates every category expectation they have.

31:04.140 --> 31:06.060
It registers as contaminated.

31:06.740 --> 31:08.740
So you cannot just serve the pasta pizza.

31:09.160 --> 31:10.280
You have to build the bridge.

31:10.720 --> 31:14.360
The baseline, step zero, is the child's safe food.

31:14.360 --> 31:17.600
A plain cracker with a slice of mild cheese on top.

31:18.140 --> 31:19.680
Let's do a slider check on step zero.

31:20.300 --> 31:21.840
The texture is crisp and dry.

31:22.400 --> 31:24.240
The flavor is mild and savory.

31:24.740 --> 31:26.400
The visual is small and square.

31:26.980 --> 31:30.440
And the context is that it's eaten with hands as a snack.

31:30.760 --> 31:33.080
Okay, what is step one in the chain?

31:33.420 --> 31:35.660
Step one is a cracker pizza.

31:36.240 --> 31:38.500
You keep the exact same crisp cracker base.

31:39.120 --> 31:41.040
You keep the exact same mild cheese.

31:41.040 --> 31:44.700
But you add a small dot of tomato sauce under the cheese.

31:45.240 --> 31:48.220
And you perhaps use a round cracker instead of a square one.

31:48.580 --> 31:55.060
Okay, looking at the vectors, we slightly bumped the flavor slider by adding the acidic tomato sauce.

31:55.360 --> 31:58.700
And we slightly bumped the visual slider by making it round.

31:58.920 --> 32:03.140
But the texture slider, the crisp snap of the cracker, is completely identical.

32:03.980 --> 32:04.460
Exactly.

32:04.900 --> 32:07.020
The family resemblance is incredibly strong.

32:07.020 --> 32:10.620
Once the child accepts step one, you move to step two.

32:11.360 --> 32:12.140
A pita pizza.

32:13.180 --> 32:13.900
What changes there?

32:14.560 --> 32:17.800
You keep the tomato sauce and the cheese exactly the same as step one.

32:18.160 --> 32:19.380
But the base changes.

32:19.960 --> 32:23.860
Instead of a crisp cracker, it is now a softer, chewy, round pita bread.

32:24.800 --> 32:26.320
The flavor vector remains anchored.

32:26.460 --> 32:29.520
But we just moved the texture slider into softer territory.

32:30.200 --> 32:32.200
Step three is an English muffin pizza.

32:32.700 --> 32:34.260
Same sauce, same cheese.

32:34.260 --> 32:36.960
But the base becomes thicker and more spongy.

32:37.780 --> 32:39.280
Moving the texture slider again.

32:40.140 --> 32:40.780
Adding volume.

32:41.680 --> 32:43.620
Step four is a flatbread pizza.

32:44.200 --> 32:46.720
The flavor core is still the identical sauce and cheese.

32:47.060 --> 32:50.140
But now the visual presentation changes dramatically.

32:50.760 --> 32:52.060
It is a large circle.

32:52.600 --> 32:54.200
And you cut it into large wedges.

32:54.400 --> 32:57.180
Rather than serving it as a small individual circle.

32:57.180 --> 33:04.480
So we just slammed the visual slider up, but anchored it with the completely familiar flavor and texture profile from the previous steps.

33:05.260 --> 33:07.440
And finally, you arrive at the target.

33:08.200 --> 33:09.260
The pasta crust pizza.

33:10.140 --> 33:15.440
Because of the previous steps, the child is now entirely comfortable with the flavor of tomato sauce and melted cheese.

33:16.280 --> 33:18.920
They are comfortable with a softer, non-crisp base.

33:19.560 --> 33:23.640
They are comfortable with the visual of a large circle cut into wedges.

33:24.140 --> 33:25.220
So they're almost there.

33:25.220 --> 33:32.780
The only slider left to move is the base texture, changing from a solid flatbread to compressed, softer pasta strands.

33:33.560 --> 33:36.440
I mean, it is a culinary heist.

33:36.620 --> 33:38.100
It is absolutely brilliant.

33:38.280 --> 33:44.180
You have slowly, methodically rewired the child's neurological definition of what a pizza can be.

33:44.560 --> 33:45.740
It's like hacking their palate.

33:46.380 --> 33:48.020
But, and this is a big but,

33:48.900 --> 33:52.900
Flyxion includes a really important, somewhat counterintuitive caveat here.

33:52.900 --> 33:56.680
It's hypothesis three, dimension specificity.

33:57.040 --> 34:03.300
And it turns out that moving the sliders in tiny, gradual increments isn't always the best strategy for every single kid.

34:03.620 --> 34:05.100
This is a vital guardrail in the paper.

34:05.780 --> 34:12.240
Our natural assumption, when we look at the math, is that smaller, finer steps between foods are always going to be more successful.

34:13.040 --> 34:14.900
A gentle slope is easier to climb than a staircase.

34:14.900 --> 34:17.500
That makes total logical sense.

34:18.080 --> 34:21.220
But Flyxion points out a fascinating psychological trap.

34:22.120 --> 34:25.960
Think back to the child with a massive sensitivity to texture, the heavy WT.

34:26.620 --> 34:31.560
For that specific child, a slow, tiny gradient of texture changes might backfire completely.

34:32.660 --> 34:35.420
It might feel like a series of unreliable near misses.

34:36.000 --> 34:38.180
Oh, like the uncanny valley of food.

34:38.180 --> 34:48.920
If I expect my favorite crisp cracker, and you give me something that looks exactly like my favorite crisp cracker, but the texture is just 5% softer,

34:49.460 --> 34:53.000
I don't think, oh, what a gentle, manageable step toward a new food.

34:53.080 --> 34:55.340
I think this specific cracker has gone bad.

34:55.400 --> 34:55.920
It is stale.

34:56.020 --> 34:56.460
It is rotten.

34:57.000 --> 34:57.380
Exactly.

34:57.720 --> 35:02.240
The expectation is violently violated, precisely because the visual similarity was so high.

35:02.240 --> 35:08.380
For that highly texture-sensitive child, the mathematical model dictates a completely different shape of chain.

35:09.040 --> 35:09.400
How so?

35:09.700 --> 35:10.420
What do you do instead?

35:10.780 --> 35:13.800
Instead of a gradual slope, you use a step function.

35:14.220 --> 35:18.680
You keep the texture slider completely, perfectly identical for as long as possible.

35:19.000 --> 35:24.240
You move from crackers to crisp toast to ultra-thin, crisp baked crusts.

35:25.100 --> 35:26.760
So you change the other sliders.

35:26.760 --> 35:27.580
Right.

35:28.180 --> 35:33.640
You change the flavor, you change the shape, but you guard that crisp texture with your life.

35:34.480 --> 35:41.860
And then, when you have exhausted those changes, you make one massive, clearly telegraphed jump in texture right at the very end.

35:42.560 --> 35:46.320
The theory doesn't prescribe a universal one-size-fits-all step size.

35:47.120 --> 35:48.920
It frames feeding as a search problem.

35:49.500 --> 35:49.980
Exactly.

35:50.200 --> 35:54.500
Where the adult has to experiment to discover the child's hidden sensory weights.

35:54.500 --> 35:59.540
Okay, the math is beautiful, but I have to push back on one of the core rules of these chains.

35:59.920 --> 36:00.560
Let's hear it.

36:00.880 --> 36:04.340
The paper insists that every step must be offered transparently.

36:04.700 --> 36:05.260
No deception.

36:06.420 --> 36:07.040
But let's be real.

36:07.240 --> 36:11.200
If I am an exhausted parent, and I just want my kid to eat some iron and vitamins,

36:11.360 --> 36:14.580
why on earth shouldn't I just puree the spinach and hide it in the brownie batter?

36:15.020 --> 36:16.100
It's tempting, isn't it?

36:16.580 --> 36:22.800
Mathematically, skipping the chain and just smuggling the target food inside a safe food is a much faster, shorter route.

36:22.800 --> 36:26.360
It gets the physical spinach into the physical stomach, yes.

36:26.720 --> 36:31.560
If your only metric is immediate caloric and mineral intake, hiding it works.

36:32.060 --> 36:34.240
Which is a valid metric on a Tuesday night.

36:34.660 --> 36:35.300
It is.

36:35.720 --> 36:39.840
But phylaxion is explicit about the long-term psychological difference here.

36:40.740 --> 36:45.180
Concealment does not expand the child's accepted cognitive categories.

36:45.540 --> 36:47.520
Because they don't know they are eating spinach.

36:47.980 --> 36:48.440
Exactly.

36:49.000 --> 36:49.840
It just tricks them.

36:49.840 --> 36:57.220
The child's mental category of spinach remains completely untouched, still sitting in the disgusting-slash-danger box.

36:57.980 --> 37:02.220
And their category of brownie is now unknowingly carrying a hidden payload.

37:03.060 --> 37:04.420
You haven't taught them to like vegetables.

37:04.600 --> 37:06.740
You've just adulterated their dessert.

37:07.120 --> 37:08.580
And if they find out.

37:08.900 --> 37:09.120
Hmm.

37:09.620 --> 37:10.720
Man, the fallout.

37:10.720 --> 37:19.240
If they discover the deception, and they usually do because kids are incredibly observant about the things they care about, it destroys trust in the caregiver.

37:19.640 --> 37:20.200
That makes sense.

37:20.860 --> 37:22.380
And what happens when trust is destroyed?

37:23.060 --> 37:26.580
We loop all the way back to process number five from the very beginning of the paper.

37:27.120 --> 37:28.840
Resistance to coercion and pressure.

37:29.340 --> 37:31.620
You spike the reactance variable.

37:31.620 --> 37:32.520
You do.

37:33.160 --> 37:37.200
You might win that specific meal, but you are losing the developmental war.

37:38.020 --> 37:43.600
The child will become even more hypervigilant and neophobic because they know you are actively trying to trick them.

37:44.040 --> 37:44.520
Wow.

37:45.340 --> 37:47.540
Okay, that is a profound shift in perspective.

37:48.340 --> 37:55.380
So, if hiding the food in the brownie is the absolute worst thing you can do for their long-term development, what is the best thing?

37:55.380 --> 37:58.580
The peeper argues that it is total transparency.

37:59.480 --> 38:01.600
And how do you achieve total transparency?

38:02.140 --> 38:02.940
Through participation.

38:03.840 --> 38:08.760
This brings us directly into the concepts of procedural familiarity and causal familiarity.

38:09.280 --> 38:10.580
Okay, let's get into that.

38:11.060 --> 38:18.240
Flyxion makes a very compelling argument that sensory familiarity, the act of just tasting and smelling a food, is too narrow of a lens.

38:18.860 --> 38:24.100
There are two other types of familiarity that are massively underutilized in overcoming neophobia.

38:24.100 --> 38:27.760
The first is procedural familiarity.

38:28.540 --> 38:32.400
Define procedural familiarity for me in a kitchen context.

38:33.040 --> 38:35.540
It is knowing how a food is obtained and prepared.

38:36.140 --> 38:40.620
It extends the child's history with the food far beyond the moment it appears on the plate.

38:41.140 --> 38:42.720
Can you give me an example?

38:43.120 --> 38:43.300
Sure.

38:43.620 --> 38:47.280
Think of a child who helps plant carrot seeds in a garden box.

38:47.640 --> 38:48.840
They water the dirt.

38:49.280 --> 38:50.740
They watch the green top sprout.

38:51.400 --> 38:53.680
Months later, they pull the carrot out of the ground.

38:54.100 --> 38:55.880
They scrub the dirt off in the sink.

38:56.140 --> 38:57.400
They watch the adult peel it.

38:57.880 --> 38:59.060
They've been with it the whole way.

38:59.400 --> 38:59.620
Right.

38:59.860 --> 39:04.460
They have immense procedural familiarity with that carrot before it ever touches their tongue.

39:04.980 --> 39:07.080
And the second type is causal familiarity.

39:07.380 --> 39:08.160
How is that different?

39:08.560 --> 39:13.060
Causal familiarity goes a step deeper into the physics and chemistry of the food.

39:13.880 --> 39:17.060
It is understanding the physical transformation of ingredients.

39:18.040 --> 39:18.380
Chemistry.

39:18.620 --> 39:18.980
Like baking.

39:18.980 --> 39:19.660
Exactly.

39:20.180 --> 39:34.280
It is knowing, through observation and participation, that this dry, powdery flour, plus this liquid water, plus this weird, bubbly yeast, will undergo a thermal transformation in the oven and turn into a loaf of bread.

39:34.720 --> 39:39.440
Okay, so planting carrots and baking bread sounds like a lovely, idyllic weekend activity.

39:39.580 --> 39:43.360
But why does this actually matter mathematically for overcoming neophobia?

39:43.360 --> 39:46.420
Neophobia, what is the mechanism in the brain that makes this work?

39:46.820 --> 39:49.440
It all goes back to the core mechanism of neophobia.

39:50.500 --> 39:50.940
Uncertainty.

39:51.740 --> 39:53.680
Neophobia is the fear of the unknown.

39:54.040 --> 39:54.160
Right.

39:54.740 --> 39:58.160
Now, imagine a finished complex meal placed in front of a child.

39:58.280 --> 40:00.080
Say, a chicken nugget or a casserole.

40:00.560 --> 40:03.900
To a child, that finished plate is an opaque, mysterious commodity.

40:04.400 --> 40:05.960
It just arrived from nowhere.

40:06.540 --> 40:08.320
A chicken nugget does not look like a chicken.

40:08.620 --> 40:10.780
It looks like a beige textured spaceship.

40:11.360 --> 40:12.280
That's hilarious.

40:12.780 --> 40:14.540
It's just a shape that appeared on the table.

40:14.760 --> 40:15.160
Exactly.

40:15.800 --> 40:21.200
If a child is neophobic, an opaque commodity is inherently terrifying because it cannot be vetted.

40:21.700 --> 40:29.120
But when a child participates in the cooking process, when they have procedural and causal familiarity, the food's history is known.

40:29.760 --> 40:30.760
The mystery is gone.

40:30.960 --> 40:31.160
Yes.

40:31.580 --> 40:36.000
The uncertainty that drives the neophobic fear is structurally eradicated.

40:36.500 --> 40:38.760
I love how Flyxion phrases this concept.

40:38.760 --> 40:43.180
But the paper says you are increasing the child's authorship over the food.

40:43.620 --> 40:46.100
They become the creator, not just the passive consumer.

40:46.500 --> 40:48.200
That's a huge psychological shift.

40:48.640 --> 40:51.720
But the paper is also very careful about the data here.

40:51.900 --> 40:56.280
We talked earlier about how passive looking doesn't work to increase taste preference.

40:56.920 --> 41:02.780
So does having a kid simply stand in the kitchen and watch you cook actually do anything to build this causal familiarity?

41:02.780 --> 41:08.520
No, and this is Flyxion's specific crucial refinement of the participation hypothesis.

41:09.520 --> 41:12.220
A lot of well-meaning advice just says, get your kids in the kitchen.

41:13.080 --> 41:19.360
But participation only works to reduce neophobia if the child actually comprehends the causal process.

41:19.360 --> 41:24.940
So what's the difference between useless participation and effective participation?

41:25.500 --> 41:36.700
If you just have a toddler stand on a stool and you hand them a wooden spoon to hold, and they have absolutely no idea what you were doing with a bubbling pot on the stove, you have not built causal familiarity.

41:37.280 --> 41:39.260
They are just standing in a room holding a piece of wood.

41:39.700 --> 41:41.100
That makes total sense.

41:41.100 --> 41:46.260
The intervention only works if the child understands the sequence of transformations.

41:46.920 --> 41:52.480
They have to see the egg crack and understand that the clear liquid turns white when it hits the heat.

41:53.220 --> 41:55.000
Comprehension is the active ingredient.

41:55.360 --> 41:58.640
That requires so much more active narration from the adult.

41:58.740 --> 42:00.700
You have to explain the chemistry as you go.

42:01.100 --> 42:01.420
You do.

42:02.360 --> 42:08.740
But Flyxion also makes this beautiful, almost relieving point about the value of doing this even when it fails.

42:08.740 --> 42:15.640
Yes. The paper explicitly warns against treating food acceptance as the singular, ultimate metric of success.

42:15.780 --> 42:15.980
Right.

42:16.420 --> 42:20.120
If you spend an hour cooking or gardening with a child, what are they learning?

42:20.620 --> 42:21.900
They are learning basic biology.

42:22.440 --> 42:24.820
They are learning sequencing and following instructions.

42:25.340 --> 42:28.840
They are learning measurement, fractions, and fine motor skills with tools.

42:29.200 --> 42:34.540
So even if you spend an hour meticulously crafting step four of the pasta pizza chain,

42:34.540 --> 42:37.640
and the child helps you roll the dough and spread the sauce,

42:37.820 --> 42:41.880
and then they sit down at the table and completely refuse to take a single bite,

42:42.560 --> 42:46.000
the intervention is still a massive educational success.

42:46.400 --> 42:46.740
Absolutely.

42:47.100 --> 42:48.100
The time was not wasted.

42:48.740 --> 42:51.340
If adults only value the caloric intake,

42:51.860 --> 42:54.120
if the only goal is getting the food down the hatch,

42:54.560 --> 42:58.440
we turn the kitchen right back into a pressure cooker of anxiety and expectations,

42:58.680 --> 43:01.280
and children smell that anxiety immediately.

43:01.280 --> 43:03.620
Okay. Speaking of pressure and expectations,

43:04.280 --> 43:08.020
letting kids cook and be authors sounds amazing in theory,

43:08.140 --> 43:10.920
but logistically, who is actually in charge of the meal?

43:11.260 --> 43:14.140
If the kid is the author, do they get to dictate the menu?

43:14.340 --> 43:15.860
Do we just eat ice cream every night?

43:16.200 --> 43:16.980
Oh, definitely not.

43:17.580 --> 43:21.600
This leads us right into the fascinating psychology of autonomy and mealtime structure.

43:22.160 --> 43:23.560
To manage this balance of power,

43:24.160 --> 43:27.580
Flyxion relies on a very famous framework in pediatric nutrition,

43:28.580 --> 43:30.580
Ellen Satter's Division of Responsibility.

43:30.580 --> 43:34.100
It is a structural blueprint for mealtimes,

43:34.560 --> 43:38.940
and it draws a hard line between the adult's job and the child's job.

43:39.500 --> 43:40.080
Give me the breakdown.

43:40.500 --> 43:43.020
The adult is strictly responsible for three things.

43:43.960 --> 43:44.800
What food is offered,

43:45.460 --> 43:46.560
when it is offered,

43:47.060 --> 43:48.580
and where it is eaten.

43:49.120 --> 43:50.760
So the adult sets the menu,

43:50.940 --> 43:51.420
the schedule,

43:51.580 --> 43:52.160
and the location.

43:52.560 --> 43:52.800
Yes.

43:53.640 --> 43:55.080
The child, on the other hand,

43:55.220 --> 43:57.480
is completely responsible for two things,

43:57.480 --> 43:59.340
whether to eat what is offered,

43:59.800 --> 44:00.900
and how much of it to eat.

44:01.500 --> 44:06.120
So it is definitely not a free-for-all permissiveness where the child runs the house.

44:06.480 --> 44:06.980
Not at all.

44:07.300 --> 44:08.780
The adult curates the environment,

44:09.140 --> 44:12.240
but the child retains absolute bodily autonomy.

44:12.240 --> 44:17.760
And Flyxion underpins Satter's framework with some heavy-hitting psychological theory.

44:18.320 --> 44:18.580
Like what?

44:19.260 --> 44:22.640
The paper brings in self-determination theory by Detsi and Ryan,

44:23.140 --> 44:25.260
and reactance theory by Jack Bream.

44:25.720 --> 44:28.440
I'm somewhat familiar with self-determination theory.

44:28.640 --> 44:33.020
It's the idea that human beings have core psychological needs for competence,

44:33.340 --> 44:34.860
relatedness, and autonomy, right?

44:35.140 --> 44:35.580
Exactly.

44:36.440 --> 44:38.520
And autonomy is the critical one here.

44:39.220 --> 44:41.680
When an adult demands that a child eat something,

44:42.620 --> 44:45.120
take two more bites of your peas before you leave the table,

44:45.640 --> 44:48.940
it is a direct threat to the child's psychological freedom.

44:49.380 --> 44:50.100
They feel trapped.

44:50.700 --> 44:53.420
Bream's reactance theory tells us that when freedom is threatened,

44:54.080 --> 44:57.480
humans will predictably rebel just to reassert their autonomy.

44:58.080 --> 44:58.840
It's human nature.

44:59.260 --> 45:00.860
If you tell me I have to do something,

45:00.860 --> 45:02.620
my immediate instinct is to refuse,

45:02.880 --> 45:05.020
even if I actually wanted to do it in the first place.

45:05.200 --> 45:05.480
Right.

45:06.160 --> 45:07.560
And in the context of a meal,

45:08.120 --> 45:12.560
the refusal becomes a psychological tool for the child to restore their freedom.

45:13.240 --> 45:15.980
The refusal is no longer about the taste of the peas.

45:16.500 --> 45:18.020
It is a negotiation of power.

45:18.400 --> 45:23.800
The museum curator metaphor really helps me conceptualize this division of responsibility.

45:24.300 --> 45:24.860
Oh, let's hear it.

45:24.980 --> 45:29.040
Satter's framework basically makes the parent the curator of an art exhibit.

45:29.040 --> 45:31.980
The curator's job is to pick the art, the food.

45:32.580 --> 45:33.860
They set the beautiful lighting.

45:33.860 --> 45:35.840
They determine the opening hours.

45:35.960 --> 45:37.560
They create a welcoming environment.

45:37.560 --> 45:44.300
But the curator cannot force the patron, the child, to stand in front of a specific painting and feel moved by it.

45:44.540 --> 45:45.120
It's impossible.

45:45.120 --> 45:50.820
If the curator runs up, grabs the patron by the back of the head and forces them to stare at a Picasso while yelling,

45:50.940 --> 45:56.220
appreciate the brushstrokes, the patron is going to hate the painting, they are going to hate the curator,

45:56.220 --> 45:58.360
and they are never going to come back to the museum.

45:58.480 --> 45:59.920
That is exactly it.

46:00.540 --> 46:03.420
You can only control the conditions under which the decision is made.

46:03.540 --> 46:05.880
You cannot control the decision itself.

46:06.440 --> 46:12.320
And this distinction becomes literally biological when we look at the difference between appetite and hunger.

46:12.320 --> 46:18.000
This is such a good distinction because you constantly hear older generations say things like,

46:18.480 --> 46:20.900
well, if they're hungry enough, they'll eat what's in front of them.

46:20.980 --> 46:21.840
Stop catering to them.

46:22.260 --> 46:27.220
Flyxion draws a sharp, crucial line here that pushes back on that old school mentality.

46:28.120 --> 46:34.120
The paper differentiates between facilitating appetite and weaponizing coercive hunger.

46:34.380 --> 46:36.420
What does facilitating appetite look like?

46:36.420 --> 46:40.840
Facilitating appetite is the adult doing their job under the division of responsibility.

46:41.500 --> 46:42.540
It's good scheduling.

46:43.260 --> 46:45.400
It means you manage the when.

46:45.820 --> 46:47.940
You don't let the child graze all afternoon.

46:48.440 --> 46:52.280
You don't give them a massive glass of milk or a dense snack an hour before dinner.

46:52.720 --> 46:56.980
You ensure they arrive at the table with a natural, healthy, biological drive to eat.

46:57.400 --> 46:57.540
Okay.

46:57.720 --> 46:59.040
And what is coercive hunger?

46:59.440 --> 47:02.580
Coercive hunger is deliberately starving a child until they comply.

47:02.580 --> 47:07.140
It's the you-will-sit-there-until-you-eat-your-broccoli-and-you-get-nothing-else approach.

47:07.540 --> 47:11.200
Which is just another form of intense pressure dressed up as discipline.

47:11.480 --> 47:11.800
It is.

47:12.060 --> 47:16.140
And biologically, it completely defeats the purpose of introducing new foods.

47:16.680 --> 47:21.880
A highly distressed, desperately hungry child is in a state of high physiological arousal.

47:22.180 --> 47:23.520
Their stress hormones are spiking.

47:23.800 --> 47:24.760
They're in fight or flight.

47:25.040 --> 47:25.440
Exactly.

47:25.440 --> 47:31.640
In that state, a human being is biologically incapable of the relaxed, open, curious mindset

47:31.640 --> 47:35.580
that is required to safely explore a novel, slightly intimidating food.

47:36.160 --> 47:39.640
You cannot be curious when you feel like you are starving and under attack.

47:39.840 --> 47:42.400
So we have all these incredible theoretical pieces.

47:42.700 --> 47:45.180
We've got the mathematical vectors of the mixing board.

47:45.380 --> 47:48.680
We've got the causal familiarity of cooking together.

47:48.960 --> 47:53.220
We've got the museum curation of Satter's framework to protect autonomy.

47:53.440 --> 47:54.240
It's a lot of theory.

47:54.240 --> 47:57.020
I can imagine a listener right now thinking,

47:57.340 --> 48:01.120
this is amazing, I am going to map out a six-step candidate chain for my kid.

48:01.360 --> 48:02.060
Yes, but.

48:02.220 --> 48:02.580
But.

48:03.260 --> 48:08.380
This brings us to what I think is the harshest but ultimately most validating and realistic

48:08.380 --> 48:10.700
reality of this entire paper.

48:11.380 --> 48:15.620
We have to talk about culinary optionality in household economics.

48:15.860 --> 48:16.160
Yes.

48:16.420 --> 48:20.980
Because building a six-step, perfectly incremented food chain requires a massive,

48:21.320 --> 48:23.420
almost overwhelming amount of household resources.

48:23.420 --> 48:24.940
It absolutely does.

48:25.760 --> 48:30.500
And to Flyxion's immense credit, the paper does not shy away from the socioeconomic reality

48:30.500 --> 48:31.900
of what it is proposing.

48:32.100 --> 48:33.280
They actually address it head on.

48:34.020 --> 48:38.520
Flyxion formalizes this concept with a concept called culinary optionality.

48:39.020 --> 48:42.120
And like the vector model, it relies on a theoretical equation.

48:42.820 --> 48:47.700
The options a household has for dinner are a product of three interacting variables.

48:47.700 --> 48:53.300
The ingredients available, the time available, and the cooking skill of the caregiver.

48:53.680 --> 48:55.080
Ingredients, time, skill.

48:55.860 --> 48:59.360
But the paper points out that skill operates differently than the other two.

48:59.720 --> 49:01.020
It operates as a multiplier.

49:01.700 --> 49:06.980
Explain how skill multiplies options without necessarily expanding the grocery budget.

49:06.980 --> 49:08.780
Let's use a very grounded example.

49:09.140 --> 49:14.260
Suppose you give a caregiver with very low cooking skill, a bag of rice, a can of black beans,

49:14.260 --> 49:15.440
and some raw chicken.

49:15.620 --> 49:15.780
Okay.

49:16.400 --> 49:19.380
They know how to boil the rice, heat the beans, and bake the chicken.

49:19.880 --> 49:21.420
So they can make one meal.

49:22.360 --> 49:23.800
Rice, beans, and chicken on a plate.

49:23.800 --> 49:30.260
If they serve that to a neophobic child, and the child rejects it, the meal is over.

49:30.660 --> 49:31.980
The caregiver is out of options.

49:32.480 --> 49:36.760
They either have to throw the food in the trash, or get up and make a separate entirely

49:36.760 --> 49:41.460
new meal, like a peanut butter sandwich, which takes more time and different ingredients.

49:41.600 --> 49:41.940
Exactly.

49:42.680 --> 49:48.580
But now take those exact same three raw ingredients, rice, beans, chicken, and give them to a caregiver

49:48.580 --> 49:49.920
with high culinary skill.

49:50.740 --> 49:52.320
Their optionality is massive.

49:52.700 --> 49:53.360
What can they do?

49:53.660 --> 49:58.400
They can make the basic plate, but if it's rejected, or if they want to build a chain,

49:58.480 --> 49:58.980
they can pivot.

49:59.800 --> 50:02.040
They can turn the rice and beans into a blended soup.

50:02.640 --> 50:04.720
They can shred the chicken and make croquettes.

50:05.320 --> 50:07.180
They can mash the beans into a patty.

50:07.180 --> 50:10.700
The high-skilled cook can create multiple vectors from the same raw material.

50:11.080 --> 50:11.300
Yes.

50:12.080 --> 50:17.000
And this introduces one of the most brilliant, practical concepts in the paper, what Flyxion

50:17.000 --> 50:23.100
calls the continuity of ingredients with sensory discontinuity, or what we in the real world

50:23.100 --> 50:25.080
might simply call the leftover loop.

50:25.280 --> 50:26.720
The leftover loop.

50:26.880 --> 50:32.080
This completely blew my mind because it solves the exhaustion problem of building these chains.

50:32.320 --> 50:33.040
It really does.

50:33.040 --> 50:38.260
If you are trying to build that pasta pizza chain, you don't have to cook six separate,

50:38.480 --> 50:43.540
exhaustive, bespoke meals from scratch just for the toddler while the rest of the family

50:43.540 --> 50:44.340
eats something else.

50:44.340 --> 50:48.200
A highly-skilled household uses material continuity.

50:49.120 --> 50:53.720
Yesterday, a large batch of beans was cooked and served as a whole bean side dish.

50:54.140 --> 50:54.720
Step one.

50:55.060 --> 51:00.460
Today, those exact same leftover beans are pureed with garlic and served as a dip with crackers.

51:00.880 --> 51:01.500
Step two.

51:01.960 --> 51:05.480
We changed the visual and texture vectors, but the material is identical.

51:06.140 --> 51:09.940
Tomorrow, the remaining purees thinned out with broth and served as a pasta sauce.

51:09.940 --> 51:10.980
Step three.

51:11.540 --> 51:17.380
Materially, nutritionally, it is the exact same ingredient moving through the week, but sensorially,

51:17.600 --> 51:19.540
it is completely discontinuous.

51:20.040 --> 51:24.540
It occupies three totally different coordinates on the child's mixing board.

51:24.880 --> 51:25.920
It is brilliant.

51:26.680 --> 51:32.680
But Flyxion includes a really stark, important warning here about what they call distributional

51:32.680 --> 51:33.060
limits.

51:33.680 --> 51:37.740
Because in our culture, society loves to shame parents for having picky kids.

51:37.740 --> 51:43.920
There is this pervasive, smug implication that if your kid only eats chicken nuggets, it's

51:43.920 --> 51:45.640
a moral failure on your part.

51:46.160 --> 51:51.360
It implies you are lazy, or you just didn't try hard enough to expose them to farmer's

51:51.360 --> 51:52.200
market vegetables.

51:52.580 --> 51:56.900
I passionately, deeply agree with Flyxion's stance in this section.

51:57.780 --> 52:03.440
Treating a narrow diet solely as a behavioral defect of the child, or a disciplinary defect of

52:03.440 --> 52:05.660
the parent, is incredibly myopic.

52:05.660 --> 52:11.660
It completely ignores the structural, economic, and systemic realities of culinary optionality.

52:12.520 --> 52:14.480
Flexibility is a privilege.

52:14.740 --> 52:17.620
It is a massive, often invisible privilege.

52:18.160 --> 52:22.920
Think about what is required to execute a leftover loop or a candidate chain.

52:23.520 --> 52:25.640
It requires abundant time.

52:26.320 --> 52:28.560
It requires physical energy at the end of the day.

52:28.880 --> 52:31.880
It requires reliable refrigeration to store the leftovers.

52:31.880 --> 52:35.760
It requires a blender or a food processor to change the textures.

52:36.340 --> 52:39.700
It requires the mental bandwidth to plan a week of interlocking menus.

52:40.040 --> 52:40.340
Exactly.

52:41.440 --> 52:46.660
A caregiver who is working multiple gig economy jobs, or caring for multiple children or elderly

52:46.660 --> 52:52.280
parents, or living in a food desert, or living in a rental unit with a broken stove, that

52:52.280 --> 52:58.140
caregiver simply cannot be expected to run multi-day, mathematically precise culinary transformation

52:58.140 --> 52:58.700
experiments.

52:58.700 --> 53:04.240
If every variation you try requires a new recipe, a new ingredient purchase that might

53:04.240 --> 53:08.620
go to waste, a new mental load, the cost of flexibility is simply too high.

53:08.620 --> 53:10.520
However, it is economically irrational.

53:11.580 --> 53:16.820
For a highly stressed, resource-constrained household, a rigid, predictable menu of a

53:16.820 --> 53:22.840
few guaranteed accepted dishes, like boxed mac and cheese or frozen nuggets, is the only

53:22.840 --> 53:23.800
rational choice.

53:24.540 --> 53:27.780
It prevents food waste, and it prevents emotional meltdowns.

53:28.480 --> 53:33.300
Fleishan explicitly states that promoting a feeding strategy that presumes abundant time

53:33.300 --> 53:37.700
and skill, and then turning around and blaming parents in constrained households for their

53:37.700 --> 53:40.340
children's narrow diets, is actively harmful.

53:41.040 --> 53:43.800
It adds moral burden to economic hardship.

53:44.300 --> 53:48.220
It's a profound point of empathy in a very dense academic paper.

53:48.500 --> 53:52.940
So we have this beautiful, empathetic, mathematically sound theory.

53:53.100 --> 53:56.920
We have vectors, chains, autonomy, and household economics.

53:56.920 --> 54:03.060
But as Fleishan reminds the reader, right now, the transformational familiarity hypothesis is

54:03.060 --> 54:05.120
exactly that, a hypothesis.

54:05.540 --> 54:10.360
It sounds great on paper, but how does the author propose we actually prove it in the real

54:10.360 --> 54:10.720
world?

54:11.400 --> 54:16.740
This brings us to the final major section of the paper, the proposed protocol.

54:17.500 --> 54:20.180
Fleishan doesn't just drop this grand theory and walk away.

54:20.520 --> 54:26.340
The paper maps out a highly rigorous, incredibly complex, six-arm randomized controlled trial.

54:27.140 --> 54:28.300
Six-arms.

54:29.080 --> 54:31.340
Most studies have a control group and a test group.

54:32.000 --> 54:34.440
Six is a massive logistical undertaking.

54:34.960 --> 54:36.520
Where do they even propose running this?

54:36.780 --> 54:41.280
They propose running it in early childhood education centers, like preschools or daycares.

54:41.720 --> 54:47.040
This is crucial because it allows the researchers to serve meals under strict experimental control,

54:47.620 --> 54:52.000
standardizing the environment and removing the emotional baggage of the home dining room.

54:52.000 --> 54:57.300
Let's break down these six-arms because looking at how they isolate the specific variables

54:57.300 --> 55:00.040
is like watching a master class in experimental design.

55:00.260 --> 55:01.180
What is arm one?

55:01.880 --> 55:03.940
Arm one is the direct presentation group.

55:04.500 --> 55:09.600
This is basically the standard exposure control group, representing the current traditional advice.

55:09.600 --> 55:16.800
The children in this arm are just offered the final target food, say the pasta crust pizza, repeatedly

55:16.800 --> 55:17.920
over several weeks.

55:18.700 --> 55:24.000
Good, enthusiastic adult modeling is used, and Satter's division of responsibility is enforced,

55:24.120 --> 55:25.100
so there's no pressure.

55:25.100 --> 55:29.620
We are just testing the old put-it-on-the-plate-until-they-eat-it method.

55:30.280 --> 55:30.620
Exactly.

55:31.380 --> 55:33.120
Arm two is where the new theory enters.

55:33.660 --> 55:35.660
This is the ordered chain passive group.

55:36.440 --> 55:41.140
These children receive the six-step sequence of preparations, moving from the cracker all

55:41.140 --> 55:43.820
the way to the pasta pizza over the course of weeks.

55:44.480 --> 55:48.780
They receive it ready-made on a plate in the mathematically correct geometric order.

55:48.780 --> 55:52.500
Okay, so arm one gets the destination over and over.

55:52.880 --> 55:55.600
Arm two gets the carefully built bridge.

55:56.120 --> 55:56.980
What is arm three?

55:57.120 --> 55:59.640
Because this one really surprised me.

56:00.220 --> 56:01.200
Arm three is brilliant.

56:01.640 --> 56:04.880
It is the randomized order chain passive group.

56:05.760 --> 56:10.400
The children in this arm receive the exact same intermediate foods as arm two, the pita pizza,

56:10.600 --> 56:15.180
the English muffin pizza, flatbread, but the foods are served in a completely scrambled random

56:15.180 --> 56:15.560
order.

56:15.560 --> 56:19.800
So instead of a smooth progression, a kid might get the thick muffin pizza on Monday,

56:20.180 --> 56:24.920
the flat cracker on Wednesday, the large flatbread on Friday, and the soft pita the next week.

56:25.300 --> 56:26.000
Why do this?

56:26.020 --> 56:27.680
It seems like it would just confuse them.

56:28.160 --> 56:29.280
That is exactly the point.

56:30.060 --> 56:32.580
Arm three exists to isolate the variable of order.

56:33.200 --> 56:33.660
Think about it.

56:33.860 --> 56:38.540
If arm three works just as well as arm two, it would mean that the geometric step-by-step

56:38.540 --> 56:40.820
progression of the vector math is actually irrelevant.

56:41.180 --> 56:45.360
It would just mean that kids simply needed exposure to a variety of ingredients.

56:46.320 --> 56:50.060
But Flyxion hypothesizes that arm two will vastly outperform arm three.

56:50.860 --> 56:54.080
They predict that the sequence matters just as much as the ingredients.

56:54.800 --> 56:58.560
Ah, it proves that the slope of the bridge matters, not just the building materials.

56:58.800 --> 56:59.160
Got it.

56:59.280 --> 57:00.160
What is arm four?

57:00.920 --> 57:03.180
Arm four introduces the participation aspect.

57:03.800 --> 57:06.340
It is the ordered chain participatory group.

57:06.340 --> 57:11.660
These children get the same correct mathematical sequences arm two, but they actively help the

57:11.660 --> 57:13.740
adults cook and assemble the food in the classroom.

57:14.500 --> 57:17.000
Testing the causal familiarity hypothesis.

57:17.640 --> 57:20.700
Does seeing the transformation reduce the neophobia?

57:21.220 --> 57:21.660
Precisely.

57:22.160 --> 57:24.060
And arm five pushes that even further.

57:24.720 --> 57:29.720
It is the ordered chain participatory plus cultivation group.

57:30.620 --> 57:31.300
Cultivation.

57:31.520 --> 57:32.080
Cultivation.

57:32.280 --> 57:33.040
Like farming.

57:33.500 --> 57:33.740
Yes.

57:34.420 --> 57:39.020
In addition to cooking the chain, these children are involved in growing or handling the raw

57:39.020 --> 57:44.220
ingredients, planting tomato seeds in a window box, harvesting basil, washing the vegetables.

57:44.960 --> 57:50.940
This tests whether combining procedural familiarity, growing, with causal familiarity, cooking,

57:51.460 --> 57:53.540
provides the ultimate reduction in uncertainty.

57:54.340 --> 57:54.680
Okay.

57:54.680 --> 57:56.940
That leaves arm six, the final group.

57:57.500 --> 57:59.960
Arm six is the ingredient matched control.

58:00.780 --> 58:04.340
In this group, the children are exposed to all the core ingredients of the target food,

58:04.460 --> 58:09.820
tomato sauce, cheese, pasta, flatbreads, but they are served in completely unrelated disparate

58:09.820 --> 58:12.700
dishes that do not form any coherent chain or prototype.

58:13.300 --> 58:19.060
So they might get plain pasta with butter one day and a grilled cheese sandwich the next day

58:19.060 --> 58:20.640
and tomato soup a week later.

58:20.860 --> 58:21.260
Exactly.

58:21.260 --> 58:24.200
This is the ultimate trap for the data.

58:24.640 --> 58:29.640
Arm six tests whether the structural similarity of the pizza prototype is the active ingredient

58:29.640 --> 58:33.140
or if just tasting the raw components in random formats is enough.

58:33.740 --> 58:36.380
It is a phenomenal experimental design.

58:36.700 --> 58:39.660
It leaves absolutely nowhere for confounding variables to hide.

58:39.880 --> 58:45.160
But the most profound part of this entire protocol, to me, isn't just about getting the kids to eat

58:45.160 --> 58:46.640
the specific pasta pizza.

58:46.640 --> 58:51.380
It's what Flyxion calls hypothesis four, the transfer hypothesis.

58:52.020 --> 58:56.860
The transfer hypothesis elevates the entire paper from a conversation about picky eating

58:56.860 --> 59:00.740
into a conversation about fundamental cognitive development.

59:01.200 --> 59:05.860
The ultimate goal of this immense effort is not just to get a child to ingest a tomato.

59:06.020 --> 59:06.580
Right.

59:06.680 --> 59:09.800
Because if you spend three months just to get a kid to eat one type of pizza,

59:09.800 --> 59:12.600
that's not exactly a scalable solution for a whole diet.

59:12.600 --> 59:13.600
Exactly.

59:13.600 --> 59:17.100
The transfer task tests what happens after the chain is complete.

59:17.100 --> 59:23.920
At the end of the trial, the researchers introduce a completely unrelated, novel, slightly intimidating

59:23.920 --> 59:25.020
food to all the children.

59:25.580 --> 59:27.840
Let's say a complex lentil curry.

59:28.660 --> 59:33.700
Flyxion hypothesizes that the children in the structured chain arms, especially the participatory

59:33.700 --> 59:38.360
ones, will show vastly higher acceptance of this totally new food than the control groups.

59:38.360 --> 59:39.360
Why?

59:39.360 --> 59:40.360
Why?

59:40.360 --> 59:42.360
They've never seen a lentil in their lives.

59:42.360 --> 59:46.640
Because navigating the chain didn't just teach them about pizza.

59:46.640 --> 59:51.120
It rewired their overarching expectation of variation itself.

59:51.120 --> 59:56.180
If we connect this to the bigger picture, this protocol is a test of how human beings learn

59:56.180 --> 59:58.040
to trust the unfamiliar.

59:58.040 --> 01:00:01.760
By walking them slowly across that bridge of vectors, you aren't just showing them that

01:00:01.760 --> 01:00:03.340
the destination is safe.

01:00:03.340 --> 01:00:06.680
You are teaching them that the act of crossing bridges is safe.

01:00:06.680 --> 01:00:07.120
Precisely.

01:00:07.680 --> 01:00:12.560
You are teaching their brain that a slight change in their environment is not a threat.

01:00:13.220 --> 01:00:14.440
It is an invitation.

01:00:15.220 --> 01:00:21.080
You are teaching them that the world is intelligible, that transformations make sense, and that they

01:00:21.080 --> 01:00:25.120
possess the agency to navigate novelty at their own pace without being forced.

01:00:25.680 --> 01:00:28.940
It's rewiring their relationship with the unknown.

01:00:28.940 --> 01:00:33.700
Okay, we have covered an immense, staggering amount of ground today.

01:00:33.700 --> 01:00:35.560
Let's trace the journey we just took.

01:00:35.560 --> 01:00:41.420
We started by entirely discarding the rigid, unhelpful, guilt-inducing label of the picky

01:00:41.420 --> 01:00:41.800
eater.

01:00:42.580 --> 01:00:49.040
We broke it down into its true components, evolutionary neophobia, sensory aversion, biological appetite,

01:00:49.360 --> 01:00:50.840
and psychological reactance.

01:00:50.840 --> 01:00:56.220
We looked at the historical literature and saw how traditional advice, just hammering kids

01:00:56.220 --> 01:01:01.920
with repetitive exposure or restricting their choices, fails catastrophically when it hits

01:01:01.920 --> 01:01:03.600
a psychological category boundary.

01:01:03.840 --> 01:01:09.260
We brought in Wittgenstein to understand family resemblance and Rosin to understand why a blue

01:01:09.260 --> 01:01:10.880
French fry feels like poison.

01:01:11.540 --> 01:01:17.600
We translated all of that into a beautiful vector equation of flavor, texture, aroma, and context,

01:01:17.600 --> 01:01:19.980
the audio mixing board of the culinary world.

01:01:20.860 --> 01:01:24.280
We mapped out the pasta pizza chain, step by careful step.

01:01:25.080 --> 01:01:30.120
We explored the deep psychological power of Satter's division of responsibility, keeping

01:01:30.120 --> 01:01:32.340
the child autonomous to prevent reactance.

01:01:33.060 --> 01:01:38.240
We discussed why causal familiarity, letting them see the opaque mystery of flour turn into

01:01:38.240 --> 01:01:42.100
the known reality of bread, cures the anxiety of the unknown.

01:01:42.100 --> 01:01:48.220
And we grounded all of this high-level theory in the harsh, pragmatic, economic reality of

01:01:48.220 --> 01:01:54.400
culinary optionality, recognizing that flexibility and the leftover loop are privileges of time

01:01:54.400 --> 01:01:58.340
and skill, and that shaming constrained parents is totally unacceptable.

01:01:58.760 --> 01:02:00.660
It is a comprehensive framework.

01:02:00.940 --> 01:02:05.360
And honestly, it is a framework that teaches us how to approach any kind of resistance to

01:02:05.360 --> 01:02:06.420
the unfamiliar in life.

01:02:06.420 --> 01:02:11.720
By building transparent, gradual, logical bridges rather than standing on the other side of

01:02:11.720 --> 01:02:14.460
a chasm and demanding immediate compliance.

01:02:14.980 --> 01:02:19.140
Whether you have children of your own, whether you work in education, or whether you are just

01:02:19.140 --> 01:02:24.140
a massive nerd for cognitive and behavioral psychology like we are, this paper fundamentally

01:02:24.140 --> 01:02:26.940
changes how you look at the world and how humans learn.

01:02:27.420 --> 01:02:34.300
But there is one final, almost throwaway detail in the paper that I cannot stop thinking about.

01:02:34.300 --> 01:02:40.080
But deep in the limits section, at the very end, Flyxion briefly mentions that adult food

01:02:40.080 --> 01:02:42.620
neophobia is heavily documented in the literature.

01:02:43.300 --> 01:02:43.720
It is.

01:02:44.220 --> 01:02:48.420
We don't magically outgrow all our category structures just because we turn 18.

01:02:48.940 --> 01:02:51.260
We carry these aversions our entire lives.

01:02:51.500 --> 01:02:51.920
Exactly.

01:02:52.400 --> 01:02:57.920
We've spent this entire deep dive talking about toddlers and childhood dinner tables, but adults

01:02:57.920 --> 01:03:00.000
have deeply entrenched food aversions too.

01:03:00.000 --> 01:03:06.060
We all have that one thing we absolutely refuse to eat, which reaves us with a final, unexamined

01:03:06.060 --> 01:03:08.120
thought for you to mull over as you go about your day.

01:03:08.780 --> 01:03:09.680
Think about your own palate.

01:03:10.360 --> 01:03:12.040
What is your pasta pizza?

01:03:12.800 --> 01:03:14.420
What is the food you reject on site?

01:03:14.540 --> 01:03:14.760
Right.

01:03:15.480 --> 01:03:17.320
Take the food you hate most in the world.

01:03:17.420 --> 01:03:18.140
Maybe it's oysters.

01:03:18.380 --> 01:03:19.380
Maybe it's mushrooms.

01:03:19.580 --> 01:03:20.360
Maybe it's blue cheese.

01:03:20.360 --> 01:03:26.880
If you took that hated food and you deliberately mapped out a six-step mathematical transformation

01:03:26.880 --> 01:03:31.840
chain, starting from your absolute favorite comfort snack, could you do it?

01:03:32.320 --> 01:03:36.020
Could you hack your own brain's categorization system to finally enjoy it?

01:03:36.520 --> 01:03:42.040
Are we all underneath it all just one perfectly calculated chain away from loving the very things

01:03:42.040 --> 01:03:42.880
we currently hate?

01:03:43.400 --> 01:03:46.580
It's a fascinating, slightly unsettling question to leave on.

01:03:46.580 --> 01:03:51.300
I'm going to go stare at some mushrooms in my fridge and do some serious vector maths.

01:03:52.480 --> 01:03:57.920
Thank you so much for taking this deep dive into the architecture of taste, biology, and

01:03:57.920 --> 01:03:59.360
curiosity with us today.

01:03:59.540 --> 01:04:00.780
It has been a wild ride.

01:04:01.360 --> 01:04:03.280
We'll see you next time the stack of sources arrives.

